    "summary": "Mellow second set with soaring Wharf Rat and thundering Phil bass",
    "blurb": "The second set opens with a heartfelt 'Morning Dew' that segues into a tight 'Playing In The Band' → 'Uncle John's Band' suite. Garcia’s guitar shines and Phil’s bass roars, while Bill Kreutzmann adds a jazzy groove on 'Mississippi Half‑Step'. The climax builds to a soaring 'Wharf Rat' before the show closes with 'Sugar Magnolia'.",
    "review": "On November 1, 1973 at McGaw Memorial Hall the Dead delivered one of their most memorable second‑set performances of the early ’70s. Starting with an emotionally charged ‘Morning Dew’, Jerry Garcia’s guitar was described as “unbelievable” and perfectly toned, leading into a seamless flow through ‘Playing In The Band’ and ‘Uncle John’s Band’. Phil Lesh’s bass sat high in the mix, thundering and propelling the band forward, while Bill Kreutzmann laid down a jazzy groove on ‘Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo’ that stands out even compared to later shows. The set peaked with a soaring ‘Wharf Rat’, showcasing Garcia’s vocal warmth, before winding down with a classic ‘Sugar Magnolia’. Both available soundboard recordings capture the band in crystal‑clear detail; the Parrillo SBD (gd73-11-01.sbd.parrillo.8834.sbeok.shnf) is especially prized for its superb instrument separation and overall balance, despite missing the first set.",
